<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capitulations of Santa Fe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-the-ottoman-empire">Capitulations</a> of Santa Fe were the agreement signed on 17 April 1492 at Santa Fe, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, between Isabella I and Ferdinand II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>. They granted Columbus the title of Admiral of the Ocean Sea, the offices of viceroy and governor of any lands he discovered, and a tenth of the profits. Disputes over these rights later led to long lawsuits between his heirs and the crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-santa-fe.jpg" alt="Capitulations of Santa Fe" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: XpoferenS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitulacionsori.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The agreement followed a form used in Catalonia since the 14th century, in which the king agreed specific conditions with those who built ships and went to sea, set out in chapters, or capítulos. The first documented capitulation dates from 1363. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s proposal was first rejected, Queen Isabella called another assembly of sailors, philosophers, astrologers and others, who judged his estimate of the distance to the Indies absurd. A group of influential courtiers, among them Hernando de Talavera, the notary Luis de Santángel and the chamberlain Juan Cabrero, persuaded the monarchs that they would lose little if the project failed and gain much if it succeeded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Juan II Coloma, secretary to Ferdinand II, was ordered to draw up the text, which took three months to prepare because the monarchs were busy with other matters. Besides the titles and the share of riches, it granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> the honorific don. It was sealed at the Santa Fe encampment, which had been built outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> as a base during the siege of the city. Columbus asked for agreements that applied only in the Catalan-Aragonese kingdom, since the Catholic Monarchs ruled their realms under their separate legal systems.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The original document has not survived. The earliest copy is in the confirmations the Crown issued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1493, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-the-ottoman-empire">Capitulations</a> were binding in Catalan law. The text does not mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, which has led some historians to suggest that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> never meant to go there, only to discover new lands. In 2009 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed the Capitulations on the Memory of the World International Register.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Capitulations of Santa Fe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its crown sponsored Christopher Columbus under the <b>Capitulations of Santa Fe</b> in 1492, divided the non-European world with Portugal by the Treaty of Tordesillas in 1494, and treated the Americas as Castilian possessions: the Council of the Indies, founded in 1524, governed them under Castilian law, and Castilian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
